ing growing scrutiny  and pressure  to prevent a mountain of confusion and  chaos on election night  rob malcolm has more america's tech  giants are bracing for what is expected  to be  an onslaught of misinformation around  election day  and stopping the spread is a major  challenge there is so much false  information out there  on all social media platforms that it's  trying to like put out a forest fire  with a garden hose  social media companies have implemented  new measures ahead of the election  to guard against false or misleading  content the companies have come to the  conclusion  that when it comes to public safety and  what comes to the integrity of the  election  they have to draw certain lines but  where they draw those lines has led to  accusations of bias  twitter's decision to block a  controversial new york post story about  hunter biden  led to a backlash especially among  republicans  thank you very much on wednesday the  ceos of facebook twitter and google got  a grilling  at the u.s center commerce committee mr  dorsey  who the hell elected you and put you in  charge of what the media are allowed to  report and what the american people are  allowed to hear  i hear the concerns and acknowledge them  but  we want to fix it with more transparency  it was a contentious hearing meant to  focus on a law that protects the ability  of tech companies  to moderate content it's a real  difficult situation for these companies  because they don't want to be accused of  censorship  they don't want to be accused of  political bias but at the same time they  don't want false information  leading their members to make bad  decisions based on  rumors that are simply not true facebook  ceo mark zuckerberg says the company  looks to be both  fair and consistent in its approach i'm  proud  that um you know our company and and  as well as the others in the industry i  think have built systems  that are very effective at this but  there are limits to what these companies  can do  and in the end it will be up to  individuals to use  their own judgment i think it's really  incumbent upon  everyone to think critically about the  information  they see and to know where to go for  validation  facebook has put together a war room to  spot election related incidents  twitter says it will flag and label any  posts that misrepresents  election results both platforms have now  banned political ads until after  the election is decided rob malcolm  global news  sacramento still ahead hurricane zeta  brings death and destruction to  louisiana  this is the impact of hurricane zeta it  destroyed a trailer in new orleans  shared the awning off a gas station and  blew a building's wall  open the storm made landfall wednesday  as a category 2 hurricane packing winds  up to 180 kilometers per hour  at least five people have died parts of  north carolina and virginia are expected  to see  damaging winds tonight in vietnam the  worst typhoon to hit the country in  20 years triggered landslides killing at  least 35 people  more than 50 others are missing and  soldiers are frantically trying to hunt  for survivors  the typhoon has damaged more than 50 000  homes and left  millions without electricity heavy rain  is expected in the region  until saturday up next the battleground  state where a unique senator  ditched his democratic dynasty  the trump and biden campaigns are both  paying close attention to the crucial  battleground  pennsylvania and luzerne county has  become something of a poster child for  the state's  swing districts it's one of a handful of  democratic strongholds that surprised  most pollsters  by going red in 2016. global's foreign  nasser spoke to a pennsylvania senator  about why he left the democratic party  to sit as an independent  meet the utics a last name that's been  synonymous with the democratic party in  pennsylvania for a generation  my father a a democratic leader here in  luzerne county for over 60 years  that's where my politics was born that's  the foundation of my politics  but for state senator john eutech 2016  caused a crack  in that foundation which kept growing  you can put  half of trump supporters into what i  call the basket of deplorables  and when you demonize someone because  they go to church on sunday because they  hunt because they're pro  second amendment or they're pro-life and  you demonize that person  and tell them they don't have value  you're not going to get their vote  i felt i no longer could be a democrat  and represent the voice of those  working-class families  now he just had to tell his elderly  staunchly democrat father  he was very proud of my decision and and  with a twinkle in his eye  he said but by god please don't become a  republican until after i die  senator udicek sits as an independent  the only one but his view isn't unique  women and men who feel they were  forgotten turned this historically blue  county red making little known luzern  county  into a national symbol of the discontent  that contributed to donald trump's 2016  victory  joe biden's been outsourcing your jobs  both candidates have been wooing lazerne  biden who hails from scranton in the  county next door held a drive-in rally  last weekend it may come down to  pennsylvania  and in luzerne county politics is a hot  topic  and he paid for what i understand at  least  200 or 300 000 in china you're listening  to fake news  fake news i just go by the facts senator  yudeck is supporting biden but thinks  trump will win  his coveted county vice president biden  is valuing the voters working class  voters  here in luzon county in a way that  secretary clinton did not  and if joe biden wins pennsylvania he'll  be the next president united states  that's what the trump camp fears and  it's why he's holding a massive rally  at the nearby airport monday the night  before  the election farrah nasser global news  washington  and that is global national for this  thursday i'm robi